[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_plan")
title = "The Mark One, against both trooper types"
question = "DOES THE NEW GUN DO THE JOB IT WAS BUILT FOR? The Mark One trades volume for power — 70 a shot on a 1.15 s bolt and a ten-round box, against the Ancient Rifle's 30 a shot on a 0.35 s trigger and thirty rounds. Sustained, that is 44 DPS against 60, so the rifle should win any fight decided by how much lead lands.

The point is the other kind of fight. A LINE trooper is 60 frame: the Ancient Rifle needs two hits, the Mark One needs one. A HEAVY is 100: the Ancient needs four, the Mark One two. So the gap between the two guns should WIDEN as the target gets tougher, and that is the whole design.

  1 vs 2   the same four troopers, both guns, against line infantry
  3 vs 4   the same four troopers, both guns, against heavies

Read pairs, not rows. If 2 beats 1 by as much as 4 beats 3, the Mark One is simply the better gun and the price is wrong. What should happen is 1 ≈ 2 and 4 > 3 — the Mark One earns its 130 against armour and pays for it against numbers.

Four a side at 40 m, six repeats, no side swap: both sides are infantry on flat ground and the arena is symmetrical."
matchups = Array[Resource]([SubResource("M1"), SubResource("M2"), SubResource("M3"), SubResource("M4")])
repeats_override = 0
speed = 6.0
